findings.body:
Any browser-supplied `Origin` is echoed in `Access-Control-Allow-Origin` while `Access-Control-Allow-Credentials` is always `true`. A malicious site can issue credentialed cross-origin requests (cookies/session) and read the responses — full CSRF-style data exfiltration against authenticated users.

CORS is enforced only by browsers; native mobile HTTP clients ignore these headers, so relaxing them does not unlock native apps. Mobile webviews still need an explicit allowlist of trusted origins (the previous `ALLOWED` set, extended with known webview origins).

Also, when `origin` is null this emits `Access-Control-Allow-Origin: *` together with credentials, which browsers reject per the CORS spec, so the null-origin path fails for credentialed calls anyway.

Restore an allowlist check before reflecting the origin, and only set `Access-Control-Allow-Credentials` for allowlisted origins:

```suggestion
  if (origin && ALLOWED.has(origin)) {
    return {
      "Access-Control-Allow-Origin": origin,
      "Access-Control-Allow-Credentials": "true",
      Vary: "Origin",
    };
  }
  return { Vary: "Origin" };
```
